#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "709ba230-de60-5640-a3b1-3fac092014b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-6x/5}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "709ba230-de60-5640-a3b1-3fac092014b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-6x/5}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302275} :answer-field/id #uuid "52bc54ab-ed20-5e27-b5c2-8e5ab134ae4a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0437cac1-0430-51b8-a73f-f9f901b83c52" :question/math-academy-id "q-332547" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=-30.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-6x/5},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-6x/5}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 0+6\\beta =-30\n$$\n\nSolving this equation gives $\\beta =-5.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-6x/5}-5.\n$$"}]}
